TOOL_RESULT read_family:
{"id": "FAM:SF-CHT-052", "document": "# FAM:SF-CHT-052 | Study family SF-CHT-052: Play-type replacement similarity vs absorb/cascade
- observed_research_question: Play-type replacement similarity vs absorb/cascade
- auto_outcome_status: inconclusive
- observed_hypothesis: Having a similar-profile teammate separates absorption from cascade when a player sits.
- observed_result: mean sim ABSORBED +0.863 vs CASCADE +0.848; Spearman(sim, -gap)=-0.19 (wrong-signed). Embiid 23-24 cascades with Reed sim 0.94.
- auto_failure_mechanism: B_powered_null (adequately powered null result)
- observed_failure_evidence: Similarity does not separate absorption from cascade; almost always findable.
- observed_sample_and_power: NOT_RECORDED
- observed_method: Exploratory map of 23-ish absences; mean replacement similarity by outcome.
- observed_verdict: null
- observed_failure_type: B_powered_null
- auto_validation_status: no_validation_recorded; evidence: NOT_RECORDED
- observed_notes: Results file on disk mentioned: absence_replacement_map.csv. Labeled non-confirmatory.
- auto_confidence: high (0.8)
- auto_members_disagree: no
- auto_knowable_before_outcome: yes_from_history
- observed_project: When a Hub Sits (Paper 2)
- observed_dates: CHT-052: 2026-07-12 (transcript)
- observed_member_records: CHT-052
- observed_sources: CHT-052: 9ea66f06-5402-4b05-850e-4b0b12a3afb6
- observed_sources: CHT-052: C:\Users\lisas\OneDrive\Documents\Basketball Strategy\paper2_output\absence_replacement_map.csv
- auto_adjudication_basis: R0 single record; automated rule-based adjudication, not human adjudication
- auto_limited_by: LIM:label_or_classification_error {basis=LIMIT_RULES on recorded power, failure evidence and notes; level=data}
- auto_has_risk: RISK:LABEL_ERROR {basis=risk taxonomy rules}
- auto_related_family: FAM:SF-APPA-005 {basis=family structure, similarity or record links; relation=limitation_precedent}
- auto_related_family: FAM:SF-CHT-032 {basis=family structure, similarity or record links; relation=limitation_precedent}
- auto_related_family: FAM:SF-CHT-086 {basis=family structure, similarity or record links; relation=limitation_precedent}
- auto_addresses_question: RQC:RQC-139 {basis=TF-IDF question clustering}
- auto_uses_method: METH:correlation {basis=keyword rules on member records}
- auto_uses_dataset: DS:Play_type_initiation_records_Neo4j_INITIATED_edges_play_type_possessions {basis=keyword rules on member records}
- auto_uses_dataset: DS:Player_absence_spells {basis=keyword rules on member records}
- auto_measures: VAR:win_probability_series_outcome {basis=keyword rules on member records}
"}

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": ["player absence", "offensive reorganization", "initiation profile", "play types", "teammate gainer", "closest pre-absence profile"],
  "methodological_questions": ["measuring similarity of pre-absence initiation profiles", "identifying largest gainer in absent player's primary play types", "handling small sample of absence spells", "confounding by role and position", "defining primary play types"],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-052",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Mean similarity ABSORBED +0.863 vs CASCADE +0.848; Spearman(sim, -gap) = -0.19 (wrong-signed). Profile similarity does not separate absorption from cascade; almost always findable. Embiid 23-24 cascaded with Reed similarity 0.94."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-052",
      "what_failed": "Using initiation-profile similarity (cosine) to predict whether a teammate absorbs or cascades the absent player's role.",
      "why": "Adequately powered null result (B_powered_null); limitation: label_or_classification_error; risk: LABEL_ERROR. Similarity almost always high and uninformative."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Cosmetic similarity of initiation profiles does not determine whether a teammate absorbs (vs cascades) the absent player's play-type role."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether the closest pre-absence initiation profile, most minutes, most initiations, or same-position minutes predicts the largest gainer in the absent player's primary play types specifically; the prior study used a different binary outcome (absorb vs cascade) and only tested similarity, on a small set of absence spells (~23)."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question mixes four candidate distance metrics without a pre-specified choice; a test that selects the best-performing metric post-hoc would be invalid.",
    "With only 11 cases (as stated in the units), statistical power is extremely low for any metric test, making a useful answer unlikely.",
    "The prior null result on similarity, combined with the small sample, strongly suggests that even if a signal exists for minutes or initiations, it would be indistinguishable from noise with 11 cases.",
    "Defining 'largest gainer' and 'primary types' requires arbitrary thresholds (e.g., top-3 play types by volume) and may produce unstable labels with few post-absence games."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact definition of '11 cases' is unclear: whether these are the only absence spells meeting a minimum-length gate, the number of teams, or a subset selected for some other reason.",
    "The granularity and completeness of per-game defensive matchup and substitution data may affect the ability to assign minutes and positions accurately.",
    "Team offensive reorganization may distribute the absent player's primary types across multiple teammates rather than concentrating them on a single 'largest gainer', reducing the question's validity."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The prior study (FAM:SF-CHT-052) tested a closely related hypothesis—that profile similarity predicts who absorbs the absent player's role—and found a clear null result. This reduces the plausibility that similarity (one of the candidate distance metrics) will work. Without that evidence, one might have considered a small-scale exploratory test; with it, the risk of another null or label-error-driven artifact is higher, reinforcing the decision not to proceed given the sample size."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.05,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Only 11 cases (out of 148 spells) are available; any meaningful test requires many more independent absence spells (≥30–40) with a clear replacement candidate."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Many teammates may have very few initiations in the absent player's primary play types, making gain estimates noisy or undefined."
      },
      {
        "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
        "text": "Defining 'largest gainer' (volume change vs percentage change, absolute vs relative) and 'primary types' (e.g., top-3 by volume) involves discretionary thresholds that may misclassify cases."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Role, minutes, opponent matchups, and lineup changes during the absence may confound any observed association between a teammate's pre-absence profile and his post-absence gain."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": ["FAM:SF-CHT-052"],
    "summary": "FAM:SF-CHT-052 partially addressed a similar question by testing whether initiation-profile similarity separates absorb from cascade outcomes; it found no effect (inconclusive, powered null). No prior work has tested the specific composite question about the largest gainer in the absent player's primary types based on multiple distance metrics."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "11 cases provide negligible power to detect any realistic effect size."
    },
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "Operationalising 'closest profile', 'largest gainer', and 'primary types' entails arbitrary cutoffs that can misclassify and create false signals."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Minutes, role, and opponent adjustments likely drive both the teammate's baseline profile and the extent to which he gains in the absent player's types, biasing naive comparisons."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Teammates with meaningful pre-absence initiation volume in the absent player's primary types may be rare, producing many missing or zero-variance comparisons."
    },
    {
      "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
      "text": "Many absences may not produce any substantial individual gainer; the outcome may be close to zero for most teammates, inflating apparent associations with any predictor."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Testing four distance metrics (profile similarity, minutes, initiations, same-position minutes) without adjustment increases the probability of a spurious finding."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.6,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.05
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The probability that, in an absence spell, the teammate who gains the most initiations (absolute volume increase) in the absent player's top-3 play types is the teammate with the highest pre-absence similarity (or most minutes/initiations/same-position minutes). A point estimate and binomial test against chance (1/k, where k = number of eligible replacement teammates) would be computed.",
    "unit": "One absence spell with a qualifying absent player (meeting minimum games missed, e.g., ≥5, and sufficient pre/post data) and at least two teammates with non-zero initiations in the absent player's primary types pre-absence.",
    "sample": "All such spells across 2022-23 to 2025-26 seasons, excluding garbage-time possessions and spells where the absent player returns during the game. The current data would provide only 11 qualifying spells; a viable test would require at least 30–40 spells, achievable with multiple additional seasons or a relaxation of the primary types definition.",
    "power": "Not computable with the current 11 spells; projected power for a one-sample binomial test with 30 spells, assuming a true hit rate of 0.4 vs chance of 0.25, would be approximately 0.3–0.4—still too low. At least 60–80 spells would be needed for 0.8 power against a moderate effect.",
    "controls": "Teammate baseline minutes, position, and role (e.g., structural influence L_pre) should be included as covariates to partial out non-similarity drivers of gain. Additionally, spell-level characteristics (games missed, strength of schedule during absence) would be controlled.",
    "falsification": "A placebo test: apply the same logic to randomly chosen non-absence periods; the association should be absent. Additionally, test whether the 'closest profile' teammate is more likely to gain in play types that the absent player never initiated; a positive result there would indicate a confound.",
    "multiplicity": "The primary test would pre-specify one distance metric (e.g., minutes) and one outcome definition; the other metrics would be reported as exploratory with Bonferroni-adjusted confidence intervals.",
    "decision_rule": "Reject the null hypothesis of random assignment (probability = 1/eligible teammates) at α = 0.05 using an exact binomial test on the pooled spells, provided the falsification tests pass.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 per-game data and derived play-type initiation records from the Neo4j graph database, with spell definitions and play-type classification as of the project's standard pipeline."
  }
}}

